  Next fork, and it's the one that constrains everything after it: [1mhow the shared validator gets loaded.[0m Right now
  [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a bare [38;5;153m<script src>[39m with globals, and [38;5;153msrc/[39m is CommonJS — there's no module system connecting them, so the
  validator's home decides whether it's importable, testable, and usable by pages you add later.

  - [1mES modules[0m ([38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, [38;5;153msrc/validation.js[39m with real exports) — one source of truth, importable by both
    the page and a Node test runner. Costs: the page must be served over HTTP (ES modules are blocked on [38;5;153mfile://[39m), and
    [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m/[38;5;153mutils.js[39m are CommonJS, so they'd convert too (2 tiny files). Hardest of the three to undo later, and
    the most standard.
  - [1mSecond script tag + global[0m ([38;5;153mvalidation.js[39m attaches [38;5;153mwindow.Validation[39m) — zero tooling, still opens by double-clicking
    [38;5;153mindex.html[39m. Costs: not importable in Node, so unit tests need a DOM harness or a dual-export hack; script ordering
    becomes load-bearing as pages multiply.
  - [1mDual export[0m (attaches to [38;5;153mwindow[39m in a browser, [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m in Node) — testable in Node with no build and no
    serving change. Costs: a boilerplate wrapper in every shared file, and it's a pattern you'd migrate off later
    anyway.

  Switching from globals to ESM later means touching every page and script tag; going the other direction is easy. Cheap
  to change afterward: the rule names and the shape of the result object.

  I recommend [1mES modules[0m. It's the only option where the validator is genuinely reusable "across the app" without each
  new page re-learning a load-order convention, and it makes plain Node unit tests possible with no harness — which
  matters because validation rules are exactly the kind of logic worth testing directly.
